// Segmented component trees: a tree whose nodes are not interchangeable
// widgets but members of a named family, where a container accepts only its
// own family's members.
//
// A canvas and its shapes are one such tree; rich text (paragraphs, inline
// runs) and menus (items, separators) are others. Nothing here knows what a
// shape is — the compiler compares declarations.
//
// A tree is a type. A component says which one it belongs to by naming that
// type in its return position, and a slot says which one it accepts by naming
// it as the slot's type. How many it accepts is the wrapper: bare is any
// number, `tree.one<T>` is exactly one, `option<T>` is none or one.
//
// Naming nothing asks: a component whose return position is empty joins the
// family its body renders, and one whose body renders members of two families
// or of none is asked to say which. Belonging to no family is `#[tree.none]`,
// and a component that says so may be placed in any tree — which is what lets
// a lifetime bracket sit in a drawing as readily as in a layout — and may
// contain a member of none. A wrapper that hosts whatever family it was
// placed in says so with a type parameter, `component boundary<T>(content
// ...component T) T`, and each call site binds T from the children it
// supplies.
